13.1  The hierarchy in 2 minutes

TL;DR
  - /etc: system-wide settings, as plain text files.
  - /home: one folder per user. /root is root's home.
  - /usr: installed programs and their files, owned by the package manager.
  - /var: data that changes: logs (/var/log), caches, databases.
  - /tmp: scratch space anyone can use.
  - /opt and /usr/local: software installed by hand.
  - /dev, /proc, /sys: devices and views of the kernel, not real files.
  - /boot: kernels and what the bootloader needs.

      man hier

Pitfalls
  - On most current distros /bin, /sbin and /lib are links into /usr ("merged
    /usr"): the same program shows up in two places.
  - Don't put your own files in /usr: the package manager owns it and an update
    can overwrite them. Use /usr/local or /opt.
  - Changing a file in /etc usually needs root, and often a restart of the
    service that reads it.
  - /var fills up quietly (logs, caches, container images): look there first
    when a disk is full.

Deep dive

  ▾ Where does this layout come from?

    Most of it dates back to early Unix, and the Filesystem Hierarchy Standard
    (FHS) now writes it down. Distros follow it, with small differences.

    Some names are accidents of history. In early Unix, /usr held the users'
    home folders; when the first disk filled up, system programs spilled onto
    that second disk too. Home folders later moved to /home, and the programs
    stayed in /usr.

  ▾ What is the merged /usr?

    Distros moved the content of /bin, /sbin and /lib into /usr and left links
    behind. Fedora did it first (release 17, 2012); Arch and Debian followed
    (Debian completed it in release 12, 2023).

    The split had lost its purpose: it existed so a small root partition could
    boot before /usr was mounted, which the initramfs now handles. With all the
    operating system in /usr, it can be mounted read-only or shared.

Related
  Commands  ls
  Paths     /etc, /usr, /var, /home
